1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,139
Every classification question on this midterm comes down to four counts.

2
00:00:05,139 --> 00:00:08,858
Get those four right, and accuracy, the true positive rate,

3
00:00:08,858 --> 00:00:12,955
the false positive rate, and the ROC curve are all just division.

4
00:00:12,955 --> 00:00:17,998
Let's build them up, then use them on real questions from the practice midterms.

5
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:21,525
Start with the confusion matrix.

6
00:00:21,525 --> 00:00:23,690
The rows are what actually happened.

7
00:00:23,690 --> 00:00:26,156
The columns are what the model predicted.

8
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:29,452
The top row holds the actual negatives.

9
00:00:29,452 --> 00:00:32,698
A true negative is a negative the model got right.

10
00:00:32,698 --> 00:00:36,138
A false positive is a negative it flagged by mistake.

11
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:39,647
The bottom row holds the actual positives.

12
00:00:39,647 --> 00:00:42,765
A false negative is a positive the model missed.

13
00:00:42,765 --> 00:00:44,908
A true positive is one it caught.

14
00:00:45,687 --> 00:00:48,015
Here's a quick way to decode the names.

15
00:00:48,015 --> 00:00:50,641
The second word is what the model predicted.

16
00:00:50,641 --> 00:00:53,864
The first word says whether that prediction was right.

17
00:00:53,864 --> 00:00:57,446
So a false negative is a negative prediction that was wrong,

18
00:00:57,446 --> 00:01:00,012
which means the case was actually positive.

19
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:03,582
Accuracy uses the whole table.

20
00:01:03,582 --> 00:01:08,670
Add up the diagonal, the cases the model got right, and divide by everything.

21
00:01:09,453 --> 00:01:14,574
The true positive rate only looks at the bottom row, the actual positives.

22
00:01:14,574 --> 00:01:18,449
Of all the positives, what fraction did the model catch?

23
00:01:18,449 --> 00:01:20,248
That's TP over TP plus FN.

24
00:01:21,020 --> 00:01:26,029
The false positive rate only looks at the top row, the actual negatives.

25
00:01:26,029 --> 00:01:30,412
Of all the negatives, what fraction did the model wrongly flag?

26
00:01:30,412 --> 00:01:32,221
That's FP over FP plus TN.

27
00:01:32,987 --> 00:01:35,124
The complements come for free.

28
00:01:35,124 --> 00:01:37,973
The true negative rate is one minus FPR,

29
00:01:37,973 --> 00:01:41,179
and the false negative rate is one minus TPR.

30
00:01:42,767 --> 00:01:46,301
Now real numbers, from practice midterm one.

31
00:01:46,301 --> 00:01:49,675
The positive class is a driver cancelling.

32
00:01:49,675 --> 00:01:55,137
On 1,013 test bookings, the logistic model gives 909 true negatives,

33
00:01:55,137 --> 00:02:00,037
69 false positives, 26 false negatives, and 9 true positives.

34
00:02:00,820 --> 00:02:05,494
Accuracy is 909 plus 9, over 1,013.

35
00:02:05,494 --> 00:02:08,031
That's about 0.906.

36
00:02:08,787 --> 00:02:11,949
TPR is 9 over 35, about 0.257.

37
00:02:11,949 --> 00:02:18,062
The 35 is every actual cancellation, the whole bottom row.

38
00:02:18,820 --> 00:02:22,467
FPR is 69 over 978, about 0.071.

39
00:02:22,467 --> 00:02:29,646
The 978 is every booking that didn't cancel, the whole top row.

40
00:02:30,420 --> 00:02:36,166
Now compare the baseline, which predicts the training set's majority class for

41
00:02:36,166 --> 00:02:36,829
everyone.

42
00:02:36,829 --> 00:02:38,965
Here, that's no cancellation.

43
00:02:38,965 --> 00:02:42,869
Its accuracy is about 0.965, higher than the model's.

44
00:02:42,869 --> 00:02:44,342
But its TPR is zero.

45
00:02:44,342 --> 00:02:47,215
It never catches a single cancellation.

46
00:02:47,987 --> 00:02:48,796
Here's why.

47
00:02:48,796 --> 00:02:53,578
Accuracy is a weighted average of TPR and the true negative rate,

48
00:02:53,578 --> 00:02:55,270
weighted by class size.

49
00:02:55,270 --> 00:02:57,918
With 35 positives and 978 negatives,

50
00:02:57,918 --> 00:03:01,743
about 97 percent of that weight is on the negatives.

51
00:03:01,743 --> 00:03:07,481
Question twelve on practice midterm three states this identity, and it's true.

52
00:03:09,067 --> 00:03:12,123
Three quick checks from the multiple choice.

53
00:03:12,123 --> 00:03:14,484
Pause if you want to answer first.

54
00:03:14,484 --> 00:03:14,762
One.

55
00:03:14,762 --> 00:03:19,624
If accuracy on the test set is exactly one, with both classes present,

56
00:03:19,624 --> 00:03:20,735
is TPR also one?

57
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:23,040
True.

58
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:27,651
Perfect accuracy means no false negatives, so every positive was caught.

59
00:03:28,420 --> 00:03:28,747
Two.

60
00:03:28,747 --> 00:03:30,137
Accuracy is 0.99.

61
00:03:30,137 --> 00:03:34,634
What's the most definitive thing you can say about TPR?

62
00:03:36,620 --> 00:03:39,442
Only that it's between zero and one.

63
00:03:39,442 --> 00:03:45,636
If one case in a hundred is positive and the model misses it, accuracy is 0.99,

64
00:03:45,636 --> 00:03:46,890
and TPR is zero.

65
00:03:47,653 --> 00:03:48,014
Three.

66
00:03:48,014 --> 00:03:51,741
True or false: changing the threshold doesn't change accuracy.

67
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:54,018
False.

68
00:03:54,018 --> 00:03:58,168
Accuracy, TPR and FPR all depend on the threshold.

69
00:03:58,168 --> 00:04:04,394
Lower it, and the model predicts more positives, so TPR and FPR both go up.

70
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,542
So where does the ROC curve come from?

71
00:04:08,542 --> 00:04:13,225
A model gives every observation a score, like a predicted probability.

72
00:04:13,225 --> 00:04:15,768
Negatives, in blue, tend to score low.

73
00:04:15,768 --> 00:04:18,511
Positives, in orange, tend to score high.

74
00:04:18,511 --> 00:04:23,060
Pick a threshold, and everything to its right is predicted positive.

75
00:04:23,820 --> 00:04:28,764
The share of the orange curve to the right of the line is the true positive rate.

76
00:04:28,764 --> 00:04:32,914
The share of the blue curve to the right is the false positive rate.

77
00:04:32,914 --> 00:04:35,722
Together, they make one point on the ROC plot.

78
00:04:36,487 --> 00:04:38,384
Now slide the threshold down.

79
00:04:38,384 --> 00:04:42,767
The model catches more positives, but it also flags more negatives,

80
00:04:42,767 --> 00:04:45,319
so the point moves up and to the right.

81
00:04:45,319 --> 00:04:49,899
A very high threshold predicts nothing positive: the point zero, zero.

82
00:04:49,899 --> 00:04:54,086
A very low one predicts everything positive: the point one, one.

83
00:04:54,086 --> 00:04:57,226
Every threshold in between traces out the curve.

84
00:04:57,987 --> 00:05:00,986
The diagonal is what random guessing gets.

85
00:05:00,986 --> 00:05:06,698
The area under the curve, the AUC, runs from 0.5 for guessing to 1 for a perfect

86
00:05:06,698 --> 00:05:07,126
model.

87
00:05:07,126 --> 00:05:12,553
It also equals the chance that a random positive scores higher than a random

88
00:05:12,553 --> 00:05:13,195
negative.

89
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:16,864
Practice midterm one, question ten.

90
00:05:16,864 --> 00:05:20,696
A doctor wants to minimize telling sick patients they're healthy,

91
00:05:20,696 --> 00:05:25,059
while wrongly telling healthy patients they're sick at most half the time.

92
00:05:25,059 --> 00:05:26,828
Which point should she choose?

93
00:05:28,387 --> 00:05:31,300
First, translate the words into metrics.

94
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:35,596
Telling a sick patient they're healthy is a false negative,

95
00:05:35,596 --> 00:05:38,363
so minimizing it means maximizing TPR.

96
00:05:38,363 --> 00:05:44,481
Wrongly alarming healthy patients at most half the time means an FPR of at most 0.5.

97
00:05:45,253 --> 00:05:51,001
Shade the allowed region, then move right along the curve as far as the limit

98
00:05:51,001 --> 00:05:54,211
allows, since TPR never falls as FPR rises.

99
00:05:54,211 --> 00:05:57,272
That's point B, with a TPR of about 0.88.

100
00:05:57,272 --> 00:05:59,511
A is allowed but gives up TPR.

101
00:05:59,511 --> 00:06:03,020
C sits on the diagonal, which is just guessing.

102
00:06:03,020 --> 00:06:04,737
And D breaks the limit.

103
00:06:06,333 --> 00:06:07,360
One more trap.

104
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:11,832
A higher AUC does not make a model better at every threshold,

105
00:06:11,832 --> 00:06:13,958
because ROC curves can cross.

106
00:06:13,958 --> 00:06:16,450
Here, model A has the larger area.

107
00:06:17,220 --> 00:06:22,459
But if you need an FPR below about 0.25, model B catches more positives.

108
00:06:22,459 --> 00:06:26,825
Question fifteen on practice midterm two tests exactly this.

109
00:06:26,825 --> 00:06:30,900
Read both curves over the range the question asks about,

110
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:34,319
and pick the one that gets there with less FPR.

111
00:06:35,933 --> 00:06:40,508
Practice midterm one, question eleven, uses two normal curves.

112
00:06:40,508 --> 00:06:44,050
Class one, the positive class, is centered at 4.

113
00:06:44,050 --> 00:06:47,740
Class two, the negative class, is centered at 5.5.

114
00:06:47,740 --> 00:06:51,208
The rule predicts positive when x is below 4.3,

115
00:06:51,208 --> 00:06:54,233
so here the positive side is on the left.

116
00:06:54,987 --> 00:07:01,232
The false positive rate is the negative class's area on the predicted positive

117
00:07:01,232 --> 00:07:03,794
side, divided by its total area.

118
00:07:03,794 --> 00:07:10,440
That's the blue area left of 4.3, which is the normal CDF at minus 1.2, about 0.12.

119
00:07:11,220 --> 00:07:16,393
The true positive rate is the same idea for the positive class.

120
00:07:16,393 --> 00:07:19,841
The orange area left of 4.3 is about 0.62.

121
00:07:20,620 --> 00:07:23,795
On the exam's lettered figure, that region is B,

122
00:07:23,795 --> 00:07:27,036
and the whole class two curve is B plus D plus E.

123
00:07:27,036 --> 00:07:28,292
So the answer is D.

124
00:07:28,292 --> 00:07:32,128
The tempting option A is actually the false negative rate.

125
00:07:33,733 --> 00:07:34,395
To recap.

126
00:07:34,395 --> 00:07:39,908
TPR uses only the actual positives, and FPR uses only the actual negatives.

127
00:07:39,908 --> 00:07:43,143
The baseline predicts the training majority,

128
00:07:43,143 --> 00:07:48,215
and on imbalanced data it can win on accuracy while catching nothing.

129
00:07:48,215 --> 00:07:54,022
Lowering the threshold raises both rates, and sweeping it traces the ROC curve.

130
00:07:54,022 --> 00:07:57,551
An AUC of 0.5 is guessing, and curves can cross.

131
00:07:57,551 --> 00:08:01,447
Under an FPR limit, take the rightmost allowed point.

132
00:08:01,447 --> 00:08:05,196
Next, we'll turn dollars into the threshold itself.
